== Exploratory Analysis and Model Performance

Principal Component Analysis (@pca) revealed substantial overlap between compound classes, with the first three principal components capturing only PC1: 5.87%, PC2: 3.53%, and PC3: 2.65% of variance respectively. The scree plot shows a gradual increase in cumulative variance with no clear elbow, reaching approximately 30% with the first 20 components, indicating that discriminative information is highly distributed across spectral features. This lack of clear class separation in low-dimensional projections suggested that linear classification methods would struggle, motivating the evaluation of both ensemble and deep learning approaches.

Three modeling strategies were systematically compared. The initial CNN architecture (@cnn1) with 320 000 parameters achieved only 23.4% test accuracy, barely exceeding random chance (20%). Training curves revealed severe overfitting, with validation loss exploding beyond 9000 while training loss remained near zero. The confusion matrix showed prediction collapse, with the model defaulting predominantly to a single class. A simplified CNN with 98% fewer parameters (@cnn2) showed 22.7% test accuracy but exhibited similar overfitting patterns with validation loss reaching 20 000, confirming that convolutional architectures were fundamentally unsuited to this sparse, high-dimensional data with limited samples.

In stark contrast, Random Forest methods substantially outperformed deep learning approaches. The baseline Random Forest achieved 64.8% test accuracy (@rf, top right), and systematic hyperparameter optimization via GridSearchCV across 648 configurations improved performance to 66.4% test accuracy, a modest but meaningful 1.6 percentage point gain. The confusion matrix (@rf, top left) revealed class-specific performance variations: Lipids exhibited the strongest diagonal (25/29 correct, 86% recall), while Heterocycles & nucleotides showed the lowest performance (9/27 correct, 33% recall), frequently confused with Nitrogenous & organic acids. Per-class accuracy analysis (@rf, bottom left) confirmed this pattern, with Lipids (86%) and Nitrogenous & organic acids (82%) performing best, and Heterocycles & nucleotides (33%) and Organic oxygen compounds (47%) performing worst. The cross-validation score distribution (@rf, bottom right) showed the optimal configuration achieved 69.3% CV accuracy with most parameter combinations clustering between 62-66%, suggesting the tuned model performance is near the achievable ceiling for this feature representation.

== Model Interpretability

Feature importance analysis (@features) demonstrated that the Random Forest learned chemically meaningful patterns. The most discriminative spectral region was 3.63 ppm (importance: 0.0170), corresponding to protons alpha to heteroatoms, characteristic of alcohols, ethers, and amino acids. The aliphatic region (1.2-1.6 ppm) dominated the top 20 features with multiple peaks (1.50, 1.60, 1.28, 1.30, 1.55, 1.53 ppm), reflecting the importance of methylene and methyl groups in distinguishing lipid structures. Aromatic signals (7.32, 6.87 ppm) also appeared prominently, indicating their role in classifying aromatic compounds. The NMR-style spectrum visualization revealed distinct importance profiles across the chemical shift range, with pronounced peaks in aliphatic (0.5-2 ppm), alpha-to-heteroatom (3-4 ppm), and aromatic (6-8 ppm) regions.

SHAP analysis (@shap_summary) provided complementary insights into feature contributions across all classes. The summary plot confirmed that 3.63, 1.60, and 7.32 ppm were the most impactful features globally, with color-coded bars indicating differential effects across compound classes. Notably, aliphatic regions (blue, Lipids) dominated the contributions for lipid classification, while aromatic regions showed strong associations with Aromatics (orange). Per-class SHAP importance (@shap_per_class) revealed class-specific discriminative regions: Aromatics relied heavily on 7.32 and 6.92 ppm (aromatic protons), Lipids on 1.60 and 1.63 ppm (aliphatic chains), Nitrogenous & organic acids on 3.63 and 1.25 ppm (alpha carbons and backbone), and Organic oxygen compounds on 3.63 ppm (protons adjacent to oxygen). These patterns align with expected chemical functional groups.